  "base_rate_reasoning": "The end-FY2024 value is 3.32%. In FY2015-FY2024, this series was sticky, with most one-year changes small relative to market-rate swings because Treasury debt reprices gradually as securities mature and are refinanced. The annual changes in the prompt average about +0.11 percentage points, with a typical move of only a few tenths. Over a two-year horizon from FY2024 to FY2026, the outside view suggests modest further drift rather than a large jump. The question background also points to CBO projections around 3.4% in 2025 and roughly 3.3% thereafter, which aligns with the structural expectation that the stock-average cost of debt moves slowly. Thus the center of the distribution should be in the low-to-mid 3% range, with upside tail risk if refinancing into higher coupons continues faster than expected and downside tail risk if market rates ease and inflation-linked costs moderate.",
