    "text": "Here are the relevant news articles:\n\n**US Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion, Threatening Global Economy and Israel**\nAccording to a report by the 'Jerusalem Post' cited by 'Sada al-Balad', US public debt has surpassed $40 trillion, creating a 'massive financial hole' that threatens to halt the global wave of interest rate cuts and impose heavy costs on other economies, including Israel. The US debt is growing at a rate of $7 billion daily, with net interest payments reaching approximately $970 billion in fiscal year 2025; the Congressional Budget Office (CBO) projects these payments will exceed $1 trillion for the first time this year, surpassing total defense spending, and could double to $2.1 trillion by 2036, representing a quarter of the federal budget. The International Monetary Fund (IMF) estimates US debt will exceed 140% of GDP by 2031. To finance the deficit, the US administration issued bonds worth $18.9 trillion from the start of 2026 through July, a 10.2% increase over the previous year, which drains global liquidity and pushes bond yields higher. Israel is already paying a price through three channels: higher interest rates increasing mortgage and loan installments; volatility on Wall Street increasing risks for Israeli retirement savings; and budget pressure forcing the state to spend billions of shekels on debt interest at the expense of education, health, and security. In a preventive measure, the regulatory body mandated financial institutions to hold a 10% foreign currency liquidity reserve. Experts like Jay Breminger of PricewaterhouseCoopers Israel warn that the technology and real estate sectors are most vulnerable, as high-risk assets lose funding when US Treasury bonds offer low-risk returns of 5%. Paul Marino of 'Themes ETFs' notes the dollar's reserve status gives the US a 'higher credit line,' while analyst Violeta Todorova of 'Leverage Shares' warns the risk lies in financing costs rather than default, as reduced foreign absorption could force the Treasury to raise yields in a vicious cycle. The Treasury has already doubled long-term bond buybacks from $2 billion to $4 billion per operation to curb yields, which reached 4.7% for 10-year bonds and 5.2% for 30-year bonds. Michael Peterson, CEO of the 'Peter G. Peterson Foundation', stated the US has run a deficit for 26 years, and every dollar paid in interest reduces citizen services and increases the likelihood of tax hikes. The report concludes that the current intervention is temporary and the final bill for the $40 trillion debt will be paid by consumers and companies worldwide through eroded wages, lower savings, and higher taxes.\nOriginal language: ar\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 12:54 PM\nSource:[صدى البلد](https://www.elbalad.news/7105671)\n\n**Global Markets Navigate Risks of Inflation, Debt, and Shifting Monetary Policies**\nGlobal stock markets are facing a convergence of economic risks as of September 2026, including persistent inflation, rising oil prices, and record-high global debt levels. According to the International Monetary Fund's reference forecast, global growth is expected to slow from approximately 3.4 percent in 2024-2025 to 3.1 percent in 2026, before rising to 3.2 percent in 2027. Total global government, corporate, household, and financial sector debt reached a record $353 trillion (approximately SR1,323.75 trillion) by the end of the first quarter of 2026, equivalent to roughly three times global gross domestic product. In the US, headline Personal Consumption Expenditures inflation stood at 3.7 percent year on year in July 2026, with core PCE at 3.3 percent, both remaining well above the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target. These conditions could compel the Federal Reserve to raise its federal funds rate above its current target range of 3.50-3.75 percent or maintain rates for longer. Consequently, longer-term US Treasury yields have risen, with the benchmark 10-year yield reaching approximately 4.78 percent and the 30-year yield at 5.25 percent. Japan's monetary policy shift also impacts global markets; the Bank of Japan has raised its policy rate to 1 percent, and the yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds has reached 3 percent. As the largest foreign holder of US Treasury securities, Japan holds approximately $1.12 trillion as of June 2026, and its rising rates may trigger an unwind of the yen carry trade. Despite these pressures, market movements on September 4 remained measured: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 0.51 percent to close at 53,414.25, the S&P 500 declined 0.38 percent to 7,718.60, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 0.29 percent to 26,506.99. While valuations in AI-related stocks and elevated interest rates present risks, investor confidence remains supported by strong corporate balance sheets and sustained consumer spending. The article concludes that while markets are absorbing pressure, the margin for error is narrowing as central banks attempt to contain inflation without significantly weakening economic growth.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 11:39 AM\nSource:[Arab News](https://www.arabnews.com/opinion/global-stocks-face-a-perfect-storm-of-risks-3001556)\n\n**Spain's Treasury to Conclude September Debt Auctions Following ECB Rate Hike**\nSpain's Public Treasury (El Tesoro Público) will conclude September's debt auctions next Thursday, following the European Central Bank's (BCE) decision to raise interest rates by 25 basis points. The BCE's Governing Council increased the deposit rate (DFR) to 2.5%, the main refinancing operation rate (MRO) to 2.65%, and the marginal lending facility rate (MLF) to 2.90%, marking the second rate hike of the year amidst persistent inflationary pressures. The upcoming auction will feature three bond types: 5-year and 7-month bonds with a 0.70% coupon (reference rate 2.722%), 8-year and 1-month bonds with a 3.45% coupon (reference rate 2.747%), and 10-year bonds with a 3.40% coupon (reference rate 3.545%). Earlier in the month, the Treasury issued its fourth syndication of 2026, a 20-year green bond maturing in July 2047, raising €4.000 billion against a demand of €68.000 billion, resulting in a subscription of over 17 times the amount issued. This green bond issuance brings the total volume of circulating green bonds to over €22.300 billion. For 2026, the Treasury forecasts new financing needs of €55.000 billion, consistent with 2025 projections. Gross total emissions are projected to reach €285.693 billion this year, a 4.2% increase compared to the 2025 forecast of €274.242 billion, driven by higher amortizations in 2026. Specifically, medium and long-term debt emissions are expected to reach €176.935 billion (a 3.1% increase), while Treasury bills are projected at €108.758 billion (a 5.9% increase).\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 07:33 AM\nSource:[Europa Press](https://ticker-noticiasep.microcontenidos.com/NoticiaRSS.vbhtml?user=J4L23F8MG9&cod=20260913091751)\n\n**Surging Global Bond Yields Signal Economic Pain Amid Inflation and Debt Concerns**\nOn September 10, 2026, yields on the longest-dated American government bonds (30-year Treasuries) climbed to nearly 5.4%, the highest level in nearly two decades, while the 10-year Treasury yield briefly reached 4.98%, according to market data reported by the Hindustan Times. This surge is driven by three primary factors: renewed conflict between Iranian and American forces in the Gulf, which pushed West Texas Intermediate oil prices from under $70 per barrel in early July to just over $100, raising inflation; a projected $1.2 trillion (3.5% of GDP) spending pledge by President Donald Trump to pay American adults $5,000 each, highlighting a disconnect between politics and macroeconomic reality; and intense private sector competition for capital, with artificial-intelligence data centers and investment-grade borrowers forecast to take out $1.9 trillion in debt this year, an increase of more than a third on 2025 levels. UBS estimates this corporate issuance has raised 10- and 30-year Treasury yields by 0.2 percentage points. In response, the US Treasury Department announced on September 9 that it would buy back up to $6 billion in long-dated bonds, a move that failed to halt the climb in yields. The phenomenon is global, with 10-year British gilts at 5.3% and German bunds at 3.5%, their highest since 2007 and 2011 respectively. Consequences include 30-year American mortgage rates rising to 7% on September 10, threats to the equity bull market, and potential strain on bank balance sheets, which held $327 billion in unrealized losses as of late June. Central banks are reacting, with the European Central Bank raising rates to 2.5% on September 10, and the Federal Reserve and Bank of Japan expected to raise rates on September 16 and 18 respectively. The US federal government's interest bill already reached $931 billion between October and July, three times the level of the same period in 2020.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 06:05 AM\nSource:[Hindustan Times](https://www.hindustantimes.com/business/surging-bond-yields-presage-pain-and-not-just-for-bond-investors-101789279353297.html)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**US Deficit Nears $2 Trillion as Interest Costs Overtake Medicare Amid Bond Market Jitters**\nAccording to a Treasury Department report released on September 11, the U.S. budget deficit reached $1.97 trillion in the first 11 months of fiscal year 2026, nearing the $2 trillion mark. August's monthly deficit was $167 billion, a 61 percent drop from July's $432 billion and a 52 percent decline from August 2025's $345 billion. Tax receipts totaled $360 billion for the month, bringing the fiscal year total to nearly $4.9 trillion, while federal spending reached $527 billion. Social Security remained the largest expenditure at $141 billion, followed by net interest payments of $86 billion. Notably, net interest costs surpassed Medicare to become the second-largest fiscal year-to-date expenditure, exceeding $1 trillion. The national debt stood at $40.048 trillion as of September 10. Market jitters followed President Donald Trump's pledge of $5,000 checks to Americans if Republicans win the November midterms, a commitment he reiterated on Truth Social. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to manage yields through debt buybacks totaling $31 billion in early September, yet 10- and 30-year Treasury yields rose to 4.97 percent and 5.36 percent, respectively. Adam Turnquist of LPL Financial noted that rates have shifted from a gradual increase to a sharp climb. Additionally, demand from primary dealers for a $22 billion 30-year bond sale on September 10 was low at $485 million, while foreign central banks purchased nearly $17.5 billion.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 12:00 AM\nSource:[www.theepochtimes.com](https://www.theepochtimes.com/business/us-budget-deficit-nears-2-trillion-amid-bond-market-jitters-6086566)\n\n**Global Markets Navigate Risks of Inflation, Debt, and Shifting Monetary Policies**\nGlobal stock markets are facing a convergence of economic risks as of September 2026, including persistent inflation, rising oil prices, and record-high global debt levels. According to the International Monetary Fund's reference forecast, global growth is expected to slow from approximately 3.4 percent in 2024-2025 to 3.1 percent in 2026, before rising to 3.2 percent in 2027. Total global government, corporate, household, and financial sector debt reached a record $353 trillion (approximately SR1,323.75 trillion) by the end of the first quarter of 2026, equivalent to roughly three times global gross domestic product. In the US, headline Personal Consumption Expenditures inflation stood at 3.7 percent year on year in July 2026, with core PCE at 3.3 percent, both remaining well above the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target. These conditions could compel the Federal Reserve to raise its federal funds rate above its current target range of 3.50-3.75 percent or maintain rates for longer. Consequently, longer-term US Treasury yields have risen, with the benchmark 10-year yield reaching approximately 4.78 percent and the 30-year yield at 5.25 percent. Japan's monetary policy shift also impacts global markets; the Bank of Japan has raised its policy rate to 1 percent, and the yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds has reached 3 percent. As the largest foreign holder of US Treasury securities, Japan holds approximately $1.12 trillion as of June 2026, and its rising rates may trigger an unwind of the yen carry trade. Despite these pressures, market movements on September 4 remained measured: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 0.51 percent to close at 53,414.25, the S&P 500 declined 0.38 percent to 7,718.60, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 0.29 percent to 26,506.99. While valuations in AI-related stocks and elevated interest rates present risks, investor confidence remains supported by strong corporate balance sheets and sustained consumer spending. The article concludes that while markets are absorbing pressure, the margin for error is narrowing as central banks attempt to contain inflation without significantly weakening economic growth.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 11:39 AM\nSource:[Arab News](https://www.arabnews.com/opinion/global-stocks-face-a-perfect-storm-of-risks-3001556)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**Global Bond Yields Rise as Markets Force Governments to Confront Debt Costs**\nGlobal bond markets are signaling a shift in investor sentiment, driving up government borrowing costs as investors demand higher yields to compensate for large debts, persistent deficits, and above-target inflation. According to data from Trading Economics as of September 2, 2025, the United States 10-year Treasury yield is 4.7850%, the United Kingdom's is 5.2255%, and Japan's has risen to 3.0180%, while China's yield has dropped to 1.6860%. The article explains that governments entered the pandemic heavily indebted and responded with massive fiscal programs, but subsequent inflation has forced central banks to maintain restrictive monetary policy. For the United States, gross federal debt recently surpassed $40 trillion, with roughly $32.3 trillion held by the public. The article notes that the average interest rate on interest-bearing Treasury debt rose from 3.32% in January to roughly 3.45% by July. The American Institute for Economic Research calculates that every sustained 5-basis-point increase in financing costs on the $32.3 trillion debt base results in approximately $16 billion more in annual interest expense. The US Congressional Budget Office (CBO) projects net federal interest expense will exceed $1 trillion in fiscal 2026, surpassing the roughly $898 billion defense budget. The article argues that rising yields are forcing governments to confront the reality that money spent servicing debt cannot be spent on other priorities like defense, healthcare, or Social Security.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 03, 2026 07:05 AM\nSource:[American Institute for Economic Research](https://thedailyeconomy.org/article/bond-markets-are-losing-patience-and-putting-governments-on-notice/)\n\n**US Public Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion Amid Rising Interest Rates and Fiscal Concerns**\nOn August 19, 2026, the public debt of the United States surpassed 40 trillion dollars (specifically $40,470,470,230,213), having more than doubled in value between 2016 and 2026, with a recent annual increase of 3 trillion dollars. According to market data, the 10-year Treasury bond rate reached 4.71 percent, the highest since 2007, while the 30-year rate hit 5.26 percent, its highest level in 19 years. The International Bourse (BIS) states that the probability of fiscal consolidation is nearly zero, noting that historical market manias often follow technological booms with capital excess, eventually leading to investment reversals and recessions. Currently, 80 percent of the debt is held by domestic agents including the Federal Reserve, foreign governments, pension funds, and private investors, while the remaining 20 percent is owed to federal agencies like Social Security. Approximately one-quarter of the debt is held by international investors and foreign governments such as Japan, the United Kingdom, and China. The estimated interest payment for this fiscal year exceeds 1 trillion dollars at an average rate of 3.5 percent, with a daily interest cost of approximately 3.3 billion dollars, resulting in a debt-to-GDP ratio of 123 percent. The article notes that aggressive fiscal policies and the economic impact of the war in Iran and rising oil prices have contributed to this scenario, while a recent attempt by the Treasury to buy back long-term bonds to lower yields failed to achieve its objective.\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: August 24, 2026 12:00 AM\nSource:[La Jornada](https://www.jornada.com.mx/noticia/2026/08/24/opinion/deuda-publica-y-el-dolar)\n\n**U.S. Treasury Debt Faces Global Competition as Yields Rise**\nRising bond yields have forced the U.S. Treasury Department into an unusual intervention, raising concerns about higher borrowing costs impacting consumer spending. The U.S. bond market, valued at $31.5 trillion as of July according to the Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association, now faces increased competition from overseas sovereign bonds; 30-year Japanese government bonds are yielding over 4%, U.K. bonds are at 5.81%, and German bonds are at 3.76%, compared to 5.27% for comparable U.S. bonds. Ira Jersey, chief U.S. interest rate strategist at Bloomberg Intelligence, stated, 'The U.S. is not the only game in town anymore.' The 10-year Treasury yield, a key benchmark for mortgages, rose to 4.74% by Friday, matching its highest point in over a year, despite Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent's announcement on Wednesday to double buybacks of longer-term bonds. This increase has pushed the average 30-year fixed-rate mortgage to its highest level in a year. The U.S. government's total debt has surpassed $40 trillion, with interest payments reaching $931 billion in the first 10 months of the fiscal year, exceeding spending on health, national defense, or veterans benefits. While yields have climbed, strategists at Macquarie Group note that default concerns have not risen excessively, suggesting a tipping point has not yet been reached.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: August 22, 2026 12:00 AM\nSource:[Fortune](https://fortune.com/2026/08/22/us-debt-competition-higher-yielding-bonds-overseas-treasury-rates/)\n\n**U.S. Debt Tops $40 Trillion, Pressuring Long-Term Treasury ETFs**\nThe U.S. government's public debt crossed the $40 trillion mark for the first time, reaching $40.047 trillion on Tuesday according to Reuters data, which includes $32.266 trillion held by the public and $7.782 trillion in intragovernmental holdings. This figure has more than doubled from $19.95 trillion when President Donald Trump took office in January 2017. The milestone coincides with the 30-year Treasury yield climbing to 5.337%, its highest level since 2007, driven by concerns over inflation, fiscal deficits, and borrowing volume. Long-duration ETFs are feeling the pressure; the iShares 20+ Year Treasury Bond ETF (NASDAQ:TLT) fell to around $81.35, its lowest closing level since 2004, and is down about 6.3% year to date. In response to the supply pressure, Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent announced on Tuesday that the government will double buyback operations for 10- to 30-year Treasury securities to at least $4 billion per operation beginning Sept. 9. Despite this, the U.S. ran a $432 billion budget deficit in July, and interest costs have climbed above $1 trillion on a rolling 12-month basis, making interest expense the second-largest federal spending category after Social Security. Consequently, investors are shifting focus toward shorter-duration Treasury ETFs, such as the iShares 1-3 Year Treasury Bond ETF (NASDAQ:SHY), to reduce interest-rate sensitivity, while long-duration funds like TLT face a test of the traditional assumption that Federal Reserve rate cuts are automatically bullish for bond prices.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: August 20, 2026 07:29 PM\nSource:[Benzinga](https://www.benzinga.com/etfs/specialty-etfs/26/08/61342497/short-duration-treasury-etfs-are-looking-safer-as-us-debt-tops-40-trillion)\n\n**US Debt Surpasses $40 Trillion Amid Market Intervention and Inflation Risks**\nAs of August 20, 2026, the US national debt has crossed the $40 trillion threshold, a milestone reached months earlier than expected, while the US stock market hit all-time highs earlier in the month. According to the article, these developments are closely linked, as the US government's fiscal deficits have funded more than half of corporate profits since 2020, driving up borrowing demand and pushing real interest rates higher. The article claims this rise reflects investor concerns over long-term government solvency rather than inflation, a sentiment exacerbated by AI hyperscalers issuing bonds for data center investments. To manage this, the US Treasury Department orchestrated a large purchase of yen to prevent the currency from weakening to the point where the Bank of Japan might sell Treasury paper, and announced an increase in repurchases of long-dated securities like the 10- and 30-year Treasury bonds to lower long-end interest rates. While these interventions caused the 10-year bond rate to fall, rates on short-term paper, such as the one-year Treasury bill and two-year Treasury note, rose. The article notes that hedge funds have stepped in to replace pension funds, with the share of US treasuries held by hedge funds nearly doubling in the last few years. This reliance on leveraged investors creates risk, as a sudden rise in short-term rates—potentially driven by inflation from the ongoing Iran war and rising oil prices—could force them to sell treasuries in a hurry. Despite these risks, the article states there is no immediate risk of a 'Liz Truss bond market meltdown moment,' though the surge in the gold price suggests some investors are preparing for potential volatility.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: August 20, 2026 11:10 AM\nSource:[UnHerd](https://unherd.com/newsroom/americas-40-trillion-debt-is-built-on-a-dangerous-gamble/?edition=us)\n\n**U.S. 30-Year Treasury Yield Hits 5.22% as Debt Concerns Mount**\nThe U.S. Treasury sold $25 billion in 30-year bonds on Thursday with a winning yield of 5.22%, the highest since August 2001, reflecting investor concerns over rising debt under the Trump administration and persistent inflation. This follows a 10-year bond auction on Wednesday with a yield of 5.06%, the highest since 2007. The U.S. national debt is approaching $40 trillion, with the debt-to-GDP ratio nearing historical highs. TD Securities U.S. interest rate strategist Gennadiy Goldberg stated, 'This is a problem for the Treasury. They have to finance the government at a higher cost.' Government debt servicing costs have doubled over the past decade and now exceed defense spending. Despite a commitment to fiscal control, the 'Big and Beautiful Act' has accelerated debt growth faster than during the pandemic. The Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget (CRFB) notes that public-held debt exceeded GDP in Q1 2026, and the Congressional Budget Office projects the debt-to-GDP ratio will surpass 106% by 2030 and reach 120% by 2036. Inflation remains a concern, with May's annual rate at 4.2% and July's at 3.4%, both above the Federal Reserve's target. The 30-year auction had a bid-to-cover ratio of 2.39, higher than the average of the previous six auctions, though analysts described it as 'demand with price conditions.' The Treasury has indicated it will not increase long-term issuance in the coming quarters, shifting toward short-term Treasury bills, which increases exposure to interest rate volatility.\nOriginal language: zh\nPublish date: August 13, 2026 11:21 PM\nSource:[UDN](https://udn.com/news/story/6811/9690731)\n\n**US Borrowing Costs Hit 25-Year High as 30-Year Bond Yields Reach 5.216%**\nThe US Treasury faced its highest borrowing costs in a quarter-century, issuing 30-year bonds at an interest rate of 5.216% on Thursday, the highest since 2001. The previous day, 10-year bond yields surged to 4.683% on $42 billion issued, the highest premium in nearly 20 years. On Thursday, the Treasury issued $25 billion in 30-year bonds. Analysts attribute these rising costs to high US debt levels, persistent inflation (CPI at 3.4%), and geopolitical risks, including the war in the Middle East. John Fath, manager at BTG Pactual AM, warned that investor demand for 30-year bonds does not appear frenzied. Gregory Faranello of AmireVet Securities noted that yields are unlikely to fall due to disproportionate deficits, solid growth, war, and inflation above central bank targets. The average cost of US debt is now over 3.45%, the highest since 2008, according to Julius Baer economist David Kohl, who described the fiscal trajectory as 'unsustainable' with a projected deficit of 6% over the next decade. The US Government Accountability Office (US GAO) estimates that approximately $4.5 trillion in bonds issued since 2009 still carry low interest rates (around 0.25%), but as these mature, the average cost of financing is expected to rise further.\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: August 13, 2026 05:33 PM\nSource:[elEconomista.es](https://www.eleconomista.es/mercados-cotizaciones/noticias/14007841/08/26/estados-unidos-se-financia-a-30-anos-al-interes-mas-elevado-en-un-cuarto-de-siglo.html)\n\n**30-Year Treasury Yields Hit 5%: Debt Service Costs Soar to $1.2 Trillion Amid Oil Shock**\nThe yield on the 30-year US Treasury bond has retaken the 5% mark, with long-end yields trading at 4.99% intraday on Tuesday, just 8 basis points from an 18-year high, while the S&P 500 remains near record territory. This divergence is driven by energy prices, as oil has risen more than 50% since late February to above $100 a barrel following conflict in the Strait of Hormuz. Bank of America strategist Michael Hartnett labels the 5% level the 'bond market’s Maginot Line,' warning that a breach could signal the end of a risk asset bull regime. The fiscal impact is severe: according to the Bureau of Economic Analysis, federal interest payments reached an annualized $1.23 trillion in Q1 2026 (4.18% of GDP), a figure the Government Accountability Office confirmed crossed $1.2 trillion in fiscal 2025. With the national debt past $39 trillion and roughly one-third of public debt rolling over annually, the Congressional Budget Office projects net interest payments will total $16.2 trillion over the next decade. Consequently, the average 30-year mortgage rate has climbed above 6.4%, according to Zillow, while Oxford Economics analyst John Canavan predicts the next rate cut timing may be pushed back from June to December due to persistent inflation.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: May 05, 2026 08:39 PM\nSource:[Benzinga](https://www.benzinga.com/analyst-stock-ratings/analyst-color/26/05/52305267/30-year-treasury-yield-5-percent-trump-interest-expense-1-2-trillion-gdp)\n\n"
