    "text": "Here are the relevant news articles:\n\n**Goldman Sachs: High Rates Won't Crash Stocks; Earnings Growth Remains Key**\nAccording to Goldman Sachs, despite the 30-year US Treasury yield surging to 5.3% and the 10-year yield nearing 5% in September 2026, a stock market crash is not inevitable. The firm argues that corporate earnings growth, rather than interest rates, is the core driver of the bull market. While the S&P 500's forward P/E ratio has compressed from 22x at the start of 2026 to 19x, the earnings yield of 5.2% remains 270 basis points higher than the real 10-year Treasury yield of 2.6%, maintaining the relative attractiveness of equities. Historical data cited by Goldman Sachs shows that while the S&P 500 averages a -2% return in the first three months of a rate-hike cycle, it rebounds to an average +9% return over the subsequent 12 months. Goldman Sachs projects S&P 500 earnings per share (EPS) to reach $340 in 2026, a 24% increase year-over-year, and rise to $385 in 2027. This growth is supported by strong corporate balance sheets, with interest coverage ratios in the 99th percentile of the last 20 years. To navigate the high-rate environment, the report advises avoiding interest-rate-sensitive sectors like homebuilders and instead focusing on financial stocks and companies with solid earnings, noting that global M&A volume has already increased by 36% year-to-date as firms invest in AI and streamline operations.\nOriginal language: zh\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 09:03 PM\nSource:[三立新聞](https://www.setn.com/news/1906521)\n\n**Global Markets Navigate Risks of Inflation, Debt, and Shifting Monetary Policies**\nGlobal stock markets are facing a convergence of economic risks as of September 2026, including persistent inflation, rising oil prices, and record-high global debt levels. According to the International Monetary Fund's reference forecast, global growth is expected to slow from approximately 3.4 percent in 2024-2025 to 3.1 percent in 2026, before rising to 3.2 percent in 2027. Total global government, corporate, household, and financial sector debt reached a record $353 trillion (approximately SR1,323.75 trillion) by the end of the first quarter of 2026, equivalent to roughly three times global gross domestic product. In the US, headline Personal Consumption Expenditures inflation stood at 3.7 percent year on year in July 2026, with core PCE at 3.3 percent, both remaining well above the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target. These conditions could compel the Federal Reserve to raise its federal funds rate above its current target range of 3.50-3.75 percent or maintain rates for longer. Consequently, longer-term US Treasury yields have risen, with the benchmark 10-year yield reaching approximately 4.78 percent and the 30-year yield at 5.25 percent. Japan's monetary policy shift also impacts global markets; the Bank of Japan has raised its policy rate to 1 percent, and the yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds has reached 3 percent. As the largest foreign holder of US Treasury securities, Japan holds approximately $1.12 trillion as of June 2026, and its rising rates may trigger an unwind of the yen carry trade. Despite these pressures, market movements on September 4 remained measured: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 0.51 percent to close at 53,414.25, the S&P 500 declined 0.38 percent to 7,718.60, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 0.29 percent to 26,506.99. While valuations in AI-related stocks and elevated interest rates present risks, investor confidence remains supported by strong corporate balance sheets and sustained consumer spending. The article concludes that while markets are absorbing pressure, the margin for error is narrowing as central banks attempt to contain inflation without significantly weakening economic growth.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 11:39 AM\nSource:[Arab News](https://www.arabnews.com/opinion/global-stocks-face-a-perfect-storm-of-risks-3001556)\n\n**Spain's Treasury to Conclude September Debt Auctions Following ECB Rate Hike**\nSpain's Public Treasury (El Tesoro Público) will conclude September's debt auctions next Thursday, following the European Central Bank's (BCE) decision to raise interest rates by 25 basis points. The BCE's Governing Council increased the deposit rate (DFR) to 2.5%, the main refinancing operation rate (MRO) to 2.65%, and the marginal lending facility rate (MLF) to 2.90%, marking the second rate hike of the year amidst persistent inflationary pressures. The upcoming auction will feature three bond types: 5-year and 7-month bonds with a 0.70% coupon (reference rate 2.722%), 8-year and 1-month bonds with a 3.45% coupon (reference rate 2.747%), and 10-year bonds with a 3.40% coupon (reference rate 3.545%). Earlier in the month, the Treasury issued its fourth syndication of 2026, a 20-year green bond maturing in July 2047, raising €4.000 billion against a demand of €68.000 billion, resulting in a subscription of over 17 times the amount issued. This green bond issuance brings the total volume of circulating green bonds to over €22.300 billion. For 2026, the Treasury forecasts new financing needs of €55.000 billion, consistent with 2025 projections. Gross total emissions are projected to reach €285.693 billion this year, a 4.2% increase compared to the 2025 forecast of €274.242 billion, driven by higher amortizations in 2026. Specifically, medium and long-term debt emissions are expected to reach €176.935 billion (a 3.1% increase), while Treasury bills are projected at €108.758 billion (a 5.9% increase).\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 07:33 AM\nSource:[Europa Press](https://ticker-noticiasep.microcontenidos.com/NoticiaRSS.vbhtml?user=J4L23F8MG9&cod=20260913091751)\n\n**US Treasury Yields Surge Past 5.3% Amid Structural Shifts and Debt Concerns**\nOn July 31, the 30-year US Treasury yield surpassed 5.27%, its highest level since 2007, and rose above 5.3% in August, while the 10-year yield approached 5%. US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent attempted to lower yields by purchasing $6 billion in bonds, but the effort failed. Meanwhile, Donald Trump has been urging Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh, whom he appointed, to cut interest rates, even asking him to 'get smart.' However, it is highly likely that Warsh and the Fed governors will raise rates next Wednesday to curb inflation. The article attributes the rise in yields to structural forces reversing a 30-year trend, including Baby Boomers drawing down savings instead of accumulating them, a decline in Chinese foreign reserves from over $4,000 billion in 2014 to approximately $3,400 billion, and geopolitical risks such as the war in Iran and frozen Russian assets. Domestically, US federal debt exceeded $40,000 billion by the end of July, with interest payments reaching $857 billion in the first nine months of the fiscal year—a 13% increase from the previous year, surpassing spending on Medicare and defense. Additionally, demand for capital to fund AI data centers is driving up borrowing costs. According to Bloomberg Economics, the natural real rate on 10-year bonds reached 2.6% in the second quarter of 2026, consistent with nominal yields around 4.7%. Jim Reid of Deutsche Bank noted that long-term 10-year yields have averaged 4.5% since 1800, suggesting current levels are a 'normalization.' Consequently, long-term Treasury ETFs, such as those from iShares, have lost 4.4% in total return over the last year, impacting American households through higher mortgage, auto loan, and credit card rates.\nOriginal language: it\nPublish date: September 12, 2026 10:35 PM\nSource:[Il Messaggero](https://www.ilmessaggero.it/economia/news/bond_usa_rendimenti-9759769.html)\n\n**Treasury Yields Surge to Multi-Month Highs Amid Expanded Repurchase Program**\nOn September 11, 2026, the 10-year Treasury yield climbed to 4.876%, its highest level since November 2023, while the two-year yield reached 4.455% and the 30-year yield rose to 5.323%. These increases occurred despite the Treasury beginning an expanded repurchase program designed to buy up to $6 billion of longer-dated debt. The article suggests that producer prices are contributing to this upward pressure on yields.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 11, 2026 10:12 PM\nSource:[InvestingChannel](https://news.investingchannel.com/article/945962/producer-prices-pour-fuel-on-the-fire-4)\n\n**Global Markets Navigate Risks of Inflation, Debt, and Shifting Monetary Policies**\nGlobal stock markets are facing a convergence of economic risks as of September 2026, including persistent inflation, rising oil prices, and record-high global debt levels. According to the International Monetary Fund's reference forecast, global growth is expected to slow from approximately 3.4 percent in 2024-2025 to 3.1 percent in 2026, before rising to 3.2 percent in 2027. Total global government, corporate, household, and financial sector debt reached a record $353 trillion (approximately SR1,323.75 trillion) by the end of the first quarter of 2026, equivalent to roughly three times global gross domestic product. In the US, headline Personal Consumption Expenditures inflation stood at 3.7 percent year on year in July 2026, with core PCE at 3.3 percent, both remaining well above the Federal Reserve's 2 percent target. These conditions could compel the Federal Reserve to raise its federal funds rate above its current target range of 3.50-3.75 percent or maintain rates for longer. Consequently, longer-term US Treasury yields have risen, with the benchmark 10-year yield reaching approximately 4.78 percent and the 30-year yield at 5.25 percent. Japan's monetary policy shift also impacts global markets; the Bank of Japan has raised its policy rate to 1 percent, and the yield on 10-year Japanese government bonds has reached 3 percent. As the largest foreign holder of US Treasury securities, Japan holds approximately $1.12 trillion as of June 2026, and its rising rates may trigger an unwind of the yen carry trade. Despite these pressures, market movements on September 4 remained measured: the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 0.51 percent to close at 53,414.25, the S&P 500 declined 0.38 percent to 7,718.60, and the Nasdaq Composite fell 0.29 percent to 26,506.99. While valuations in AI-related stocks and elevated interest rates present risks, investor confidence remains supported by strong corporate balance sheets and sustained consumer spending. The article concludes that while markets are absorbing pressure, the margin for error is narrowing as central banks attempt to contain inflation without significantly weakening economic growth.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 11:39 AM\nSource:[Arab News](https://www.arabnews.com/opinion/global-stocks-face-a-perfect-storm-of-risks-3001556)\n\n**Spain's Treasury to Conclude September Debt Auctions Following ECB Rate Hike**\nSpain's Public Treasury (El Tesoro Público) will conclude September's debt auctions next Thursday, following the European Central Bank's (BCE) decision to raise interest rates by 25 basis points. The BCE's Governing Council increased the deposit rate (DFR) to 2.5%, the main refinancing operation rate (MRO) to 2.65%, and the marginal lending facility rate (MLF) to 2.90%, marking the second rate hike of the year amidst persistent inflationary pressures. The upcoming auction will feature three bond types: 5-year and 7-month bonds with a 0.70% coupon (reference rate 2.722%), 8-year and 1-month bonds with a 3.45% coupon (reference rate 2.747%), and 10-year bonds with a 3.40% coupon (reference rate 3.545%). Earlier in the month, the Treasury issued its fourth syndication of 2026, a 20-year green bond maturing in July 2047, raising €4.000 billion against a demand of €68.000 billion, resulting in a subscription of over 17 times the amount issued. This green bond issuance brings the total volume of circulating green bonds to over €22.300 billion. For 2026, the Treasury forecasts new financing needs of €55.000 billion, consistent with 2025 projections. Gross total emissions are projected to reach €285.693 billion this year, a 4.2% increase compared to the 2025 forecast of €274.242 billion, driven by higher amortizations in 2026. Specifically, medium and long-term debt emissions are expected to reach €176.935 billion (a 3.1% increase), while Treasury bills are projected at €108.758 billion (a 5.9% increase).\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 13, 2026 07:33 AM\nSource:[Europa Press](https://ticker-noticiasep.microcontenidos.com/NoticiaRSS.vbhtml?user=J4L23F8MG9&cod=20260913091751)\n\n**US 30-year Treasury Yield Hits 5.34% as Oil Prices Fuel Rate-Hike Bets**\nOn Thursday, September 10, 2026, US Treasury yields surged as a sharp rise in oil prices reignited inflation fears, pushing the 30-year Treasury yield to 5.34%, its highest level since 2007, according to CNBC TV18. The surge, driven by benchmark oil prices jumping more than 4% to their highest level since May, led traders to raise the odds of a Federal Reserve rate hike next week to around 70%, with markets now pricing in a rate increase by October rather than December. Across maturities, yields rose by six to eight basis points, with the two-year yield climbing above 4.5% for the first time since 2024. The pressure extended globally, with UK two-year gilt yields rising more than 10 basis points and euro-zone bond markets weakening following the European Central Bank's decision to raise its deposit rate by 25 basis points to 2.5%. Investors are awaiting Friday's US consumer price index data, while the US Treasury announced a buyback of up to $6 billion in 10- to 20-year debt, tripling the initial target, though the yield surge suggests market focus remains on the broader inflation and rate-policy outlook.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 10, 2026 02:14 PM\nSource:[cnbctv18.com](https://www.cnbctv18.com/market/bonds/us-30-year-treasury-yield-hits-5-34-highest-since-2007-as-oil-fuels-rate-hike-bets-19988725.htm)\n\n**Caputo Expects Lower Rates as Treasury Auctions Short-Term Debt Amid Stable Market Conditions**\nOn September 10, 2026, Argentine Economy Minister Luis Caputo is preparing for a Treasury debt auction that reflects a context of stable interest rates near 20% annualized. According to the article, the total debt maturities due are $8.1 trillion pesos, which is lower than the average of $9 trillion pesos in the previous five auctions. This favorable position is supported by Treasury deposits in the Central Bank of $7.8 trillion pesos as of September 4 and a recent absorption of $3 trillion pesos in repos by the Central Bank, creating excess liquidity. The auction menu includes instruments maturing between November 2026 and June 2027, specifically a Lecap (S13N6), two TAMAR instruments (TMF27, TML27), a CER (X29E7), and four dollar-linked bonds (D30O6, D30N6, D31M7, TZV27). Notably, the Treasury is avoiding issuing debt maturing within the next presidential term to separate debt discussions from electoral uncertainty. Market indicators show stability, with the cautionary rate at 18% annualized and the wholesale dollar closing at $1514 (a 0.2% increase). The article anticipates a rollover exceeding 100% and notes that the upcoming August inflation data from INDEC is expected to be around 1.6%, which could further support a decline in interest rates and credit costs.\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: September 10, 2026 12:26 PM\nSource:[El Cronista](https://www.cronista.com/finanzas-mercados/caputo-preve-tasas-mas-bajas-emite-deuda-pero-a-corto-plazo/)\n\n**US 30-Year Mortgage Rates Hit 6.85% Amid Rising Yields and Inflation Fears**\nAccording to the Mortgage Bankers Association, the average 30-year mortgage rate for Americans reached 6.85% in the week ended September 4, 2026, marking a rise of 6 basis points (bp) and the highest level in over 14 months. This increase reflects rising yields on US government debt, with the 10-year Treasury yield approaching 4.8%, its highest since October 2023. Contributing factors include tensions in the Middle East, which have pushed up oil prices and inflation fears, alongside concerns about US federal debt, which topped 40 trillion dollars in August. Higher borrowing costs have begun to dampen demand, with refinancing applications falling 6.2% and total mortgage applications declining 2.7% over the same week. Markets are now leaning toward a rate hike rather than a hold following the Federal Reserve meeting on September 15 and 16, pushing back prospects for cheaper mortgage credit.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 09, 2026 02:11 PM\nSource:[Market Screener](https://www.marketscreener.com/news/real-estate-americans-are-now-borrowing-at-6-85-for-30-years-ce785bd9de8cf424)\n\n**US 30-Year Mortgage Rates Hit 6.85% Amid Inflation and Debt Concerns**\nAccording to the Mortgage Bankers Association, an American purchasing a home with a 30-year mortgage is now borrowing at an average rate of 6.85%, a figure that rose by 6 basis points for the week ending September 4, 2026. This rate represents the highest level in over 14 months, driven by rising US Treasury yields, with the 10-year Treasury rate approaching 4.8%, its highest point since October 2023. The article attributes this increase to tensions in the Middle East, which have pushed oil prices higher and fueled inflation fears, alongside concerns regarding the US federal debt, which exceeded $40 trillion in August. Consequently, the housing credit market is contracting: refinancing applications fell by 6.2% in a single week, while total mortgage applications (including purchases and refinances) dropped by 2.7%. Looking ahead, the article notes that upcoming inflation data and the Federal Reserve meeting scheduled for September 15-16 will be critical, with markets increasingly pricing in a rate hike rather than a pause, delaying the prospect of cheaper credit.\nOriginal language: fr\nPublish date: September 09, 2026 01:23 PM\nSource:[zonebourse](https://www.zonebourse.com/actualite-bourse/immobilier-un-americain-emprunte-desormais-a-6-85-sur-30-ans-ce785bd9de88fe24)\n\n**Why September Remains Wall Street's Cursed Month Amid $40tn Debt and AI Capital Crunch**\nThe article analyzes why September is historically a weak month for Wall Street, noting that since 1950, the S&P 500 has averaged a -0.68% return with only a 44% probability of gains, making it the only month to finish in the red more often than green. While no single event defines this 'September effect' unlike the October crashes of 1929 or 1987, the article attributes the trend to post-vacation portfolio reassessments, fiscal year-end rebalancing, and a heavy slate of economic releases including Federal Reserve meetings. The core concern for September 2026 is not just the seasonal trend but a convergence of high bond yields and massive debt issuance: US public debt crossed the $40tn threshold on August 19, 2026, with Treasury data showing a $40.08tn figure shortly after. In the July-September 2026 quarter alone, the Treasury projects $739bn in new net marketable issuance. This supply surge, combined with 10-year Treasury yields at 4.7% and 30-year yields topping 5.2%, pressures growth stocks, particularly in the AI sector where giants like Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft are projected to spend up to $770bn in capital in 2026. This competition for capital creates a delicate loop where rising yields increase interest costs—already $1,170bn for the federal government in fiscal 2026 through July—potentially driving a market rotation from speculative growth to profitable, cash-generating firms. Key market-moving dates include the August jobs report on September 4 (following a July loss of 23,000 jobs), the August CPI on September 11, the Federal Reserve meeting on September 15-16 (where the policy rate sits between 3.50% and 3.75%), and the August PCE data on September 30 (with July reading at 3.7%).\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: September 01, 2026 01:47 PM\nSource:[Market Screener](https://www.marketscreener.com/news/september-wall-street-s-cursed-month-ce7858ddd08ff721)\n\n**Spanish Treasury Announces September Auctions with Record Interest Rates for 2026**\nThe Spanish Treasury (Tesoro Público), an entity dependent on the Ministry of Economy, will return to the markets on Tuesday, September 2, 2026, to auction 6-month and 12-month Treasury bills (Letras del Tesoro) with a target of raising between 5.500 and 6.500 million euros. This follows a gap since August 11, 2026, when the last issuance occurred. For this upcoming auction, the reference marginal interest rate for 6-month bills is set at 2.512%, the highest level since January 2025, while the rate for 12-month bills is 2.679%, the highest since September 2024. Following this, the Treasury will return on Thursday, September 3, 2026, to auction government bonds and obligations targeting 5.250 to 6.750 million euros. These include 3-year bonds (2.35% coupon), 5-year bonds (2.60% coupon), 9-year 8-month obligations (3.30% coupon), and 15-year inflation-indexed obligations (2.05% coupon), with marginal yields of 2.871%, 3.008%, and 1.724% respectively. Looking ahead, the Treasury plans to return on September 8 and September 17, 2026. The Ministry of Economy forecasts new financing needs of 55.000 million euros for 2026, consistent with 2025 projections. This includes 50.000 million euros for medium-to-long-term debt and 5.000 million euros for Treasury bills. Gross total emissions are projected to reach 285.693 million euros in 2026, a 4.2% increase over the 2025 forecast of 274.242 million euros, driven by higher amortizations in 2026. Specifically, medium-to-long-term debt gross emissions are expected at 176.935 million euros (up 3.1% from 2025's 171.514 million), while Treasury bill emissions are projected at 108.758 million euros (up 5.9% from 2025's 102.728 million).\nOriginal language: es\nPublish date: September 01, 2026 08:18 AM\nSource:[Las Provincias](https://www.lasprovincias.es/economia/letras-tesoro-inversores-rentabilidad-20260901101744-nt.html)\n\n**Treasury Yields Fall as July Jobs Report Dashes September Hike Bets**\nU.S. Treasury yields fell on Friday after July payrolls unexpectedly dropped by 23,000 jobs, compared to a forecast for 80,000 job gains, according to data reported by Reuters. The unemployment rate eased to 4.1% as labor participation fell, and average hourly earnings rose 3.2% on the year, below the consensus 3.5% increase. Tom di Galoma, managing director of global rates trading at Mischler Financial Group, stated that the weak labor market 'takes the Fed off the hiking table.' Consequently, the odds of a Federal Reserve interest-rate hike in September fell to 44% from 55%. The 2-year note yield dropped 4.35 basis points to 4.202%, reaching its lowest since July 17, while the benchmark 10-year note yield fell 1.44 basis points to 4.656%. Despite the weakening jobs data, traders maintain a 77% probability of a rate increase by December. The Treasury Department is scheduled to sell $125 billion in coupon-bearing debt next week, including $58 billion in 3-year notes, $42 billion in 10-year notes, and $25 billion in 30-year bonds. Market volatility is also being influenced by concerns over the Iran conflict and upcoming consumer price inflation data for July.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: August 07, 2026 07:28 PM\nSource:[mint](https://www.livemint.com/market/treasury-yields-fall-as-jobs-report-dashes-hike-bets-11786130890731.html)\n\n**U.S. Debt Hits $39 Trillion: Three TIPS ETFs to Hedge Persistent Inflation**\nAccording to a U.S. Treasury Department report, the gross national debt reached $37.6 trillion as of September 30, 2025, with real-time tracking from April 2026 placing the figure at $39 trillion. The article argues that the government has a motive to allow inflation to run 'modestly hot' to manage the $10 trillion in debt maturing in 2026, noting that interest payments on the debt were approximately $970 billion in 2025. To hedge against this persistent inflation, which currently sits between 2.8% and 3% against a 2% target, the article highlights three Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) ETFs. SCHP is presented as a core holding with a 0.05% expense ratio and an effective duration of 6.5 years, offering broad exposure but facing duration risk. VTIP is described as a conservative option with a weighted average maturity of 2.5 years and a 0.07% expense ratio, making it less sensitive to rate movements. LTPZ is identified as a high-conviction, high-risk play on long-duration TIPS that could benefit from falling real rates but suffered double-digit losses in 2022. The article concludes by noting that while the Schwab U.S. TIPS ETF holds a 'Hold' rating, top analysts at MarketBeat have not included it in their top five recommended stocks.\nOriginal language: en\nPublish date: April 19, 2026 12:45 PM\nSource:[Market Beat](https://www.marketbeat.com/stock-ideas/39-trillion-debt-signal-3-tips-etfs-to-hedge-persistent-inflation/)\n\n"
