   "reasoning": "1. **Hypotheses**\n- **H0 (status quo / central):** FY 2026 average interest rate ends up near the recent trend, roughly **3.2%–3.6%**.\n- **H1 (lower):** It comes in **below 3.2%**.\n- **H2 (moderately higher):** It comes in **3.6%–4.0%**.\n- **H3 (much higher):** It comes in **above 4.0%**.\n\n2. **Evidence vs hypotheses**\n- **E1 [F6]** and **E2 [F6]**: These mostly say the resolving figure is not yet available / not verified. That is consistent with all hypotheses and contradicts none in a meaningful way. Very low weight.\n- **E3 [A1]**: Confirms the Treasury monthly dataset/methodology. This supports using the historical series and the exact metric; neutral among H0/H1/H2/H3.\n- **E4 [A1]**: The metric excludes TIPS and FRNs from those averages. This matters a bit because it means the series is somewhat smoother and less sensitive than some broader “all-in” borrowing-cost intuitions. Slight support for **H0** over extreme H2/H3.\n- **E5 [A1]**: Confirms fiscal-year timing and date handling. Methodological only; neutral among levels.\n\nThe strongest substantive evidence is actually the historical path in the prompt plus the CBO projection in the background. Those favor **continued gradual drift** rather than a sharp jump.\n\n3. **What must change before resolution**\nFor any specific non-status-quo value, what has to happen is simply that Treasury publishes the observation on or closest to **2026-09-30**. That is near-certain. The real uncertainty is the underlying Sep 2026 stock-average rate.\nBetween now and 2026-09-30, a big move away from H0 would require either:\n- much faster rollover of low-coupon debt into high coupons than expected,\n- sustained higher Treasury yields through the year,\n- or an unexpectedly large effect from debt composition.\nBecause this is a **stock average** over total interest-bearing debt, those forces usually transmit slowly. So large deviations in either direction are possible but not the default.\n\n4. **Update from blind base rate**\nThe blind base rate was centered in the low-to-mid 3s:\n- p40 3.36\n- p60 3.52\n- p80 3.78\n\nI update only slightly upward from that center. Why upward? By FY2024 the series had risen to **3.324%**, and the debt stock was still rolling out of very low-rate vintages from 2020–2021. Even if market yields stabilized or eased somewhat, the stock-average cost could still drift upward into FY2026. Against that, CBO’s broad projection of around **3.3%–3.4%** argues against a large increase. Net: modest upward nudge, still close to the outside view.\n\nSo I move the median from roughly **3.44%** blind to about **3.50%**.\n\n5. **Forecast**\nMy median forecast is **3.50%**. Distribution remains fairly tight because this series is sticky, but with a meaningful right tail in case refinancing pressure keeps lifting the average faster than expected.\n\nFINAL: 3.50\n\n```json\n{\"percentiles\": {\"5\": 3.00, \"10\": 3.08, \"20\": 3.20, \"40\": 3.39, \"60\": 3.58, \"80\": 3.84, \"90\": 4.03, \"95\": 4.18}}\n```",
